The real DER encoding happens on lines 117-121 via `writeDerSeq(...)`. The two statements above are pure dead code that allocates four throwaway ByteArrayOutputStreams for no purpose. They should be removed."}, {"file": "authz/client/src/main/java/org/keycloak/authorization/client/util/crypto/ASN1Decoder.java", "line": 49, "severity": "low", "category": "bug", "title": "readSequence silently mishandles indefinite-length and over-long TLVs", "body": "`readSequence()` does two unsafe things:
1. If `readLength()` returns -1 (BER indefinite-length encoding, which `readLength()` explicitly supports), the `while (length > 0)` test is false on entry and the method returns an empty list while leaving the end-of-contents marker and the entire sequence body unread. Callers will then see a zero-length list and either index out of bounds or silently accept an empty signature.
2. `readNext()` can in principle return more bytes than the declared remaining length (malformed input); in that case `length` underflows, the loop exits, and the leftover bytes are silently swallowed instead of being rejected as malformed.
The reader should either reject indefinite-length as not supported for DER or read until the 0x00 0x00 end-of-contents octets, and should validate that the consumed bytes never exceed the declared length."}, {"file": "common/src/main/java/org/keycloak/common/crypto/CryptoIntegration.java", "line": 69, "severity": "low", "category": "style", "title": "logger.debugf called with a plain string and no format arguments", "body": "`logger.debugf(builder.toString());` passes a String with no `%` placeholders and no varargs to `debugf(String, Object...)`. This is misleading because the signature suggests a printf-style format string. If any discovered CryptoProvider class name ever contained a `%` (unlikely but not enforced), it would be interpreted as a format specifier and could throw. Use `logger.debug(builder.toString())` (or `logger.tracef` if formatting is desired) instead."}, {"file": "authz/client/src/test/java/org/keycloak/authorization/client/test/ECDSAAlgorithmTest.java", "line": 42, "severity": "low", "category": "test_gap", "title": "Test uses a single P-256 key for ES256/ES384/ES512", "body": "The constructor calls `KeyPairGenerator.getInstance("EC").genKeyPair()`, which on the standard SunEC provider returns a P-256 (secp256r1) key. That same key is then used to exercise ES256, ES384 and ES512, each of which expects a different curve and a different raw signature length (64, 96, 132 bytes respectively). With a P-256 key the SHA384withECDSA/SHA512withECDSA signatures still have only 32+32 byte components, so `getSignatureLength()` returns 96/132 while the real signature is 64 bytes; the test only round-trips the bytes through DER<->concatenatedRS and so happens to pass even though no ES384/ES512-shaped signature is ever produced. The test does not verify the conversion for genuinely larger curves and does not assert that a signature produced by `signature.sign()` round-trips to the original signature value for the expected length. Generate (or load) a key of the appropriate curve per algorithm and assert the round-tripped bytes actually equal the original `sign()` output."}]}

findings.body:
Lines 114-115 create two ASN1Encoder instances and call `write()` on each, then discard the results without ever consuming the produced bytes:
```
ASN1Encoder.create().write(rBigInteger);
ASN1Encoder.create().write(sBigInteger);
```
The real DER encoding happens on lines 117-121 via `writeDerSeq(...)`. The two statements above are pure dead code that allocates four throwaway ByteArrayOutputStreams for no purpose. They should be removed.
